{"name":"Batch Brew","hex":"#45392f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"batch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The quantity of bread or other baked goods baked at one time.","A quantity of anything produced at one operation."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To aggregate things together into a batch.","To handle a set of input data or requests as a batch process."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/batch"},{"term":"brew","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make tea or coffee by mixing tea leaves or coffee beans with hot water.","To heat wine, infusing it with spices; to mull."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The mixture formed by brewing; that which is brewed; a brewage, such as tea or beer.\n\n A serving of beer.","A serving of beer."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brew"}],"entries":[{"title":"Drip coffee","blocks":[{"text":"Drip coffee is made by pouring hot water onto ground coffee beans, allowing it to brew while seeping through. There are several methods for doing this, including using a filter. Terms used for the resulting coffee often reflect the method used, such as drip-brewed coffee, or, somewhat inaccurately, filtered coffee in general. Manually brewed drip coffee is typically referred to as pour-over coffee. Water seeps through the ground coffee, absorbing its constituent chemical compounds, and then passes through a filter. The used coffee grounds are retained in the filter, while the brewed coffee is collected in a vessel such as a carafe or pot."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/TEDxAMS2011_044.JPG/330px-TEDxAMS2011_044.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drip_coffee","wikidata":"Q1155703"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Drip_Coffee_Bangkok.jpg/500px-Drip_Coffee_Bangkok.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=